# The sweep that could not say what failed: full gate output persisted, and every red test named under its own target (2026-10-05)

== 1. What the sweep actually kept

A full 44-gate sweep (`bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build`) run on 2026-10-05 finished with one red gate. This
is the entire trace the runner produced for it -- 4 lines for the gate, plus the summary line:

```
PASS  cargo check
PASS  cargo clippy
FAIL  cargo test (exit 101)
    
    running 0 tests
        `-p chaos-engine --lib`
PASS  GUI protocol types

FAILED gates: cargo test
44 gate(s) run, 0 skipped, 1 failed
```

`running 0 tests` is a doctest leg of an unrelated crate. The name of the failing test appeared nowhere: the runner
printed `tail -n 40` of the gate's output and the complete output lived only in a shell variable, so it disappeared
when the process exited. Which test went red was, at that moment, unknowable from the artifact the sweep leaves
behind.

The reason the tail is systematically the wrong end of the output is the gate's own command line
(`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:405`):

```
  gates+=("cargo test: ${bootstrap}; cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast")
```

`--no-fail-fast` makes cargo keep going after the first failing target and defer its blame line to the very end:

```
error: 1 target failed:
    `-p chaos-engine --lib`
```

So a test leg's output has the failing test names in the middle and the failing target flags at the end, and a
40-line tail can carry at most one of the two -- in practice it carried the target flag and lost the name.

== 2. The change

`scripts/verify-gates.sh` now does three things on a red gate:

1. writes the gate's **complete** output to a file and names that file in the failure block and in the summary;
2. lifts cargo's own names for what failed out of the whole output rather than out of what fits on screen;
3. folds them into one line per red test, tagged with the target cargo was running when it printed it.

The directory defaults to `target/verify-gates/<UTC timestamp>-<pid>/` and can be overridden with `--log-dir DIR` or
`VERIFY_GATES_LOG_DIR`. Files are named `NN-<slug>` where `NN` is the gate's ordinal in the run, and the slug is
filtered to `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` so a label with shell metacharacters cannot escape the directory.

Deviation from the wording of the TODO row this closes: the row asked for `$TARGET_DIR/gate-logs/<slug>.log`. The
directory is instead per-run and timestamped, because two sweeps on the same tree would otherwise overwrite each
other's logs -- and the case that motivated this (a sweep whose log had to be compared against a later rerun of the
same command) is exactly the case where overwriting loses the evidence. The ordinal prefix keeps the files in gate
order inside the run directory.

Measured against the capture in section 4, the failure block now reads:

```
    target(s) cargo blames, as it says them:
      -p chaos-engine --lib
    red, 1 of them, one line each, tagged with the target cargo was running:
      [Running unittests src/lib.rs (target/debug/deps/chaos_engine-80b8ed7d12d00ecb)] tests::headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint
    full output of this gate: <scratch>/b26/replay-logs/01-the-leg-under-replay (302 lines)
```

The attribution comes from cargo's own per-target header (`     Running unittests src/lib.rs (...)` / `   Doc-tests
<crate>`) printed above the `failures:` block. Names are matched as bare identifiers, plus one tightly anchored shape
for doc-tests, because cargo does not name a doc-test by an identifier -- it names it `src/lib.rs - add (line 3)`,
which the first version of this code silently dropped. That shape was taken from a real cargo run rather than
assumed: a throwaway crate with one deliberately failing doc-test produced

```
   Doc-tests doctest_probe

running 1 test
test src/lib.rs - add (line 3) ... FAILED
...
failures:
    src/lib.rs - add (line 3)

error: doctest failed, to rerun pass `--doc`
```

A guard whose findings are indented prose therefore gets no fabricated test list, which is what keeps the identifier
rule from being quietly loosened into a line-matcher.

== 3. Self-test and mutation matrix

`bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test` went from 60 cases to **96/96 pass**. The new fixtures are a cargo-shaped
output generator with three targets (`Running` x2 plus `Doc-tests`), 300 filler lines placed above the failure block
so the buried-name property is real rather than merely asserted, and an indented-prose guard used as the negative
control.

The matrix below mutates one behaviour at a time and passes only if every mutation turns at least one self-test case
red, the mutant still parses as bash, and the tree comes back byte-identical (sha256). Mutations G10-G12 aim
specifically at attribution: a report that names the right tests but files them under the wrong target is as
unusable as one that names none, and G1-G9 cannot see the difference. Verbatim transcript:

```
