G9 is reported for completeness as a case where the first version of the driver was wrong rather than the code: the
empty-value half of the `--log-dir` guard had no case behind it, so the mutation legitimately survived until the case
`an empty --log-dir value is refused, not parsed` was added.

== 4. Replay of verbatim cargo bytes

The self-test fixture is authored, so on its own it only proves the code agrees with an author's idea of cargo's
shape. This driver therefore captures a genuine failing run of the real gate command, then replays those exact bytes
through the shipped runner as a gate (`cat` of the capture plus the captured exit status), so the reporting path
exercised is the shipped one and the input is cargo's own output.

The capture is a flake, so the driver loops until a genuinely failing run appears and aborts rather than fabricating
one if none shows up. The run quoted below caught it on attempt 5 of 40; capture sha256
`cd7259dc88234dceefbecfbc270462dd867d1a1691257fd82699954116c2bf80`, 302 lines. Verbatim:

```
== capture attempt 1
== capture attempt 2
== capture attempt 3
== capture attempt 4
== capture attempt 5
== captured a failing run on attempt 5: 302 lines
== real bytes: 302 lines, printed tail would start at line 263
== first `failures:` test-name line: 222
== cargo's target-failed line:       301
== PROVEN: the test name is 263 lines above the printed tail's first line
== replay exit: 1
   45:        `-p chaos-engine --lib`
   46:    target(s) cargo blames, as it says them:
   47:      -p chaos-engine --lib
   48:    red, 1 of them, one line each, tagged with the target cargo was running:
   49:      [Running unittests src/lib.rs (target/debug/deps/chaos_engine-80b8ed7d12d00ecb)] tests::headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint
   50:    full output of this gate: /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/b26/replay-logs/01-the-leg-under-replay (302 lines)

== verdict
== the target cargo blamed (-p chaos-engine --lib) is the target the replay printed
== the name cargo printed (tests::headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint) is printed under the target cargo printed it in
== the persisted file holds cargo's whole output (only trailing blank lines differ)
ALL CHECKS PASS
```

The distance the fix buys is the number in that transcript: the failing name is on line 222, the old printed tail
would have begun on line 263, so the name sat **41 lines above** everything the previous runner showed anybody.

The panic itself, from the same capture, verbatim:

```
---- tests::headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint stdout ----

thread 'tests::headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint' (1407915) panicked at crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs:4091:41:
called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: "无法启动 headless Agent: Text file busy (os error 26)"
note: run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace
```

== 5. The intermittents this makes visible

Four intermittent failures are now named by the runner instead of being lost. All four exist at HEAD and none of
them is in code this batch touched.

| test | site | observed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `tests::headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint` | `crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs:4091:41` | `Result::unwrap()` on `Err("无法启动 headless Agent: Text file busy (os error 26)")` |
| `remote::forward::tests::a_grant_that_runs_out_closes_the_listener` | `crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/remote/forward.rs:770` | `the local port was still held after the grant ran out` |
| `process_resources::tests::thread_gauge_tracks_spawned_threads` | `crates/codegen/xai-tty-utils/src/process_resources.rs:386` | gauge out of step with spawned threads |
| `tests::armed_child_dies_when_parent_exits` | `crates/codegen/xai-tty-utils/src/lib.rs:1611` | `grandchild pid <n> outlived its parent despite kill_on_parent_death_std (PR_SET_PDEATHSIG not effective)` |

Measured rates, on this host, for the `chaos-engine`/`xai-tty-utils` pair:

| how it was run | result |
| --- | --- |
| one test alone, 12 repeats | 12/12 pass |
| prebuilt suite, `--test-threads=8`, 15 then 20 repeats | 2/15 red, then 1/20 red |
| `cargo test --no-fail-fast --lib -p chaos-engine -p xai-tty-utils`, 25 attempts | 4 non-green |
| same command, 40-attempt budget, two separate launches | red on attempt 1; red on attempt 26 |
| same command, this batch's capture loop | red on attempt 5 |

The failure is therefore load- and timing-dependent, not a property of the test body: the same binary passes in
isolation and loses roughly one run in twenty to one in five under concurrency.

== 6. Not claimed

- The root cause of `Text file busy (os error 26)` on the headless Agent spawn is **not** diagnosed here. The
  observability change was deliberately landed first, because the TODO row's own reasoning applies: any "fix" for an
  intermittent whose name nobody can see is a guess. The leading hypothesis is that a freshly written test binary is
  still being closed (`fput`/`i_writecount` still pinned) at the moment `execve` runs on it, or that the busy file is
  the `/bin/sh` interpreter of the child rather than the binary; `strace -f -e trace=execve` on a red run is the
  planned instrument. `gdb` and `ltrace` are not installed on this host.
- The doc-test naming rule is proven against a real doc-test failure captured from cargo, and against the fixture.
  It is not proven end-to-end inside a full `cargo test --workspace` leg, because that leg was green on every attempt
  this batch made.
- The buried-name property is measured on a 2-target capture (41 lines). For the real 44-gate leg, where the test leg
  spans the whole workspace, the gap is larger, not smaller: the sweep log in section 1 kept 38 lines of an unrelated
  crate's doctests and no name at all.
- The self-test proves the extraction and the persistence. It does not prove that a reader who follows the printed
  pointer finds a useful file on a filesystem where `target/` is later wiped; `target/` is a build directory and is
  cleaned by the same cargo that fills it.
